summoned to war  these armenian reservists are leaving  the capital girovan  president nicole pashanyan called all  men into military service  after fighting erupted on sunday over  the disputed province of  nagorno-karabakh  they will fight their neighbours  azerbaijan for control of the territory  [Applause]  on the front line armenia says it is  bombarding azeri  forces and winning what both sides are  calling a war  the last time they fought a full war was  in the early 1990s after the collapse of  the soviet union  thirty thousand people were killed a  ceasefire was broken in 1994  but sporadic fighting has continued ever  since  nearly three decades later armed with  twitter  cell phone and drone videos information  is a new front line  in the first 24 hours of fighting  armenia and azerbaijan released videos  and boasted of their military  superiority each accused the other of  killing civilians  and of hiring mercenaries from syria and  the wider middle east  to bolster their forces both have also  released videos of soldiers they have  killed  claiming casualties the other denies  russia the eu  and the us are calling for calm while  turkey  is backing azerbaijan  i want to declare that not only  azerbaijan but also  turkey is at war with us they are using  the main modern weapons and ammunition  available in the turkish army drones  and aircraft  hospitals in nagorno-karabakh have  received dozens of wounded soldiers and  civilians  they have moved some patients into bomb  shelters  we have 30 wounded and there are  operations undergoing some patients who  were in hospitals were evacuated into  the bunker  and the seriously ill people are in the  hospital we're working on high alert and  are ready to receive the wounded  azerbaijan said the violence has spread  outside of nagorno-karabakh  with five family members killed in the  city of naftalan  many more were hurt as the casualty  figures climb  armenia and azerbaijan are pushing on  both appear determined to settle  who rules nagorno-karabakh using weapons  and words charlotte ballas al jazeera  we are across this story from three  different locations this hour alexandre  estonovich godfather is in  uh moscow with reaction from there we've  also got cinema kosiolu in baku  azerbaijan  starting with robin forrester walker  though following events  from the georgian capital uh to belissi  take us through  more of what day two has brought  well now we have the armenian defense  ministry  confirming that a total of 59  soldiers have so far been killed  uh and and dozens more wounded  civilians on both sides as charlotte was  referring to more civilian  casualties because the civilian  uh areas the villages and towns that are  dotted along  uh the front lines or the line of  control  are never too far away from the conflict  and those  missiles and munitions that are being  used are not as accurate  as the drone footage that we're seeing  so much of on the military  units appears to show so that is what's  been unfolding  militarily more fighting more soldiers  and more civilians killed in with the  armenians giving those  figures we're not yet hearing about what  the actual casualty  uh rate is looking like from the azeri's  side  um also important to add into this mix  the as armenians now uh asserting that  turkey is involved in this conflict that  turkey has been providing  uh its uh military experts and its  equipment  from f-16 fighter planes to drones but  again  this is an information war and we have  not been able to verify that  independently  and we've also heard again today from  turkey  uh president erdogan saying that he  believes the osce mince group the body  the international body that was tasked  with finding a way  out of this with a peaceful solution  since the 1990s  has failed in its task and that  azerbaijan  rightfully deserves to take back its  territories that belong to azerbaijan  all right robin forrester walker with  that update from georgia in tbilisi  let's move to baku now  uh azerbaijan here is cinema kosovo  yusun and i believe you've been to a  government briefing what are they saying  well yes as soon as we landed in bucky  we went to a government briefing by the  presidential  advisor the former presidential  spokesperson  by the defense ministry so uh basically  the  uh if you look at the number of the  casualties here on the azeri side  the defense ministry claims that 20  civilians have been  injured and six civilians have been  killed but they are not giving  any numbers on the military casualties  on the azerite signs but they are  saying that the armenians who are the  invaders  of the nagano nagorno-karabakh and for  the azerbaijan government  have been attacking the civilian areas  mainly the villages  and there is a fight there is a very  tense fight around tartar city which is  the most populated  uh area in the nagorno-karabakh and the  surrounding villages yesterday we  learned that  the azerites took back seven villages  they recaptured them from the armenian  side  but today uh villagers uh today fighting  is ongoing  10 kilometers to the fuso city which is  very close to  tartar the most populated area we have  been told by our colleagues that  prior to this uh attack from the  armenian side and the military operation  by the azeri's  those villages were in a way evacuated  by the government mostly women have been  evacuated from uh the area because they  were expecting that  attack and we have heard from the  presidential advisory matajian that  uh the houses were attacked  by mortar shells from the armenian side  for instance he gave an example of a  family  where five children with five children  two of them died  ambulances have been attacked also a  carpet school where  uh women in the area go there to learn  how to sow a carpet and sell it to earn  money actually  that building has been attacked so  actually the discourses from  by by both the armenian and the  azerbaijan side are  almost similar come out because they  both accuse each other of  invasion they both accuse each other of  propaganda we asked about the  mercenaries  the foreign mercenaries to presidential  advisor and he said that  azerbaijan doesn't need any mercenaries  their military capacity is enough to  deal with this conflict  in the border but they said that he said  that actually  armenians were bringing mercenaries from  syria and lebanon  to the uh to the contact area as they  say  and where the conflict is ongoing and  since they are not registered under the  military service  the the death number is not being  counted this is what he said  uh also the defense ministry  spokesperson  underlined such an information as well  but  as robin also put through turkey is  involved in this conflict because turkey  has a military base in azerbaijan  and turkey has been involved in this uh  issue between armenia and azerbaijan for  decades come up  and a turkey has been supporting the  azeri military with all the  defense industry equipment it has been  producing as well  but the turkish defense ministry also  issued a statement  making a call that uh armenia who has  invaded invaded  nagorno-karabakh should immediately  leave that area  and stop bringing foreign mercenaries  from the middle east  with that update from baku in azerbaijan  finally  we go to moscow here's alexandre  stonovich  russia with this very important role to  play not so much about taking science  here but about mediating  yes kamal exactly russia is putting  itself  forward as a mediator if there is willed  for a mediation of course saying that  they can use  uh their good relations that they have  with both armenia and azerbaijan to help  resolve this conflict they also warned  third parties from  interfering they called on them to  restrain as well they call uh  the parties involved uh to refrain from  any steps that can  make the situation even more difficult  saying that the situation is sliding  into a war between armenia  and azerbaijan now also france and eu  which are also with russia members of  the osce minsk  group are say are calling for immediate  ceasefire  to going back to the negotiating table  in difference to that turkey of course  is  as we heard uh both from robin and sinan  siding with azerbaijan  and where they are seeing the solution  to this problem is  that armenia should leave the azeri  territories uh  also the disputed area of  nagorno-karabakh  turkish president recep tayyip erdogan  said as robin pointed out  that minsk group actually failed for  decades to resolve this problem so now  it's time for that azerbaijan  to solve it for themselves and in the  line  with uh russian calls for ceasefire and  the calls for  actually the whole international  community for the ceasefire is  also the reaction from the high  representative of eu joseph pharrell who  said  that this should all return to the under  the auspices  of the osce means group so the group  co-chaired with with russia  france and united states alexandre  sternovich  with that report from moscow thank you  for that  right so we're going to put all of this  on the map because it is a region which  i think is  new for a lot of people you hear the  name over and over again the contested  nagorno-karabakh region we've shaded it  in green here  this is the heart of the standoff  between armenia and azerbaijan  nagorno-karabakh itself is  internationally recognized as  part of azerbaijan but the majority  of the populations so we're talking  about these red areas here  they are ethnic armenian and are  controlled by armenian forces  then we add in the shaded areas here the  orange and white areas  also recognize the area recognized as  azerbaijan's territory  but armenia controls it and then you  have  those over there the sort of small  pockets in blue they're controlled by  azerbaijan  now we'll add one more thing in here  which is the so-called line of contact  which separates here it is the green  line separates the armenian forces and  the azerbaijan armed forces  in nagorno-karabakh all this fighting  you've seen  it's taking place all along this green  line  well let's hear from thomas de val now  who is the senior fellow at carnegie  europe  specializes on the caucasus region we  spoke to him and he says that  despite the region's international  importance the outside community remains  disengaged from this dispute  unfortunately this round of fighting  promises to be the worst probably since  since the ceasefire and of course  the war of the 1990s was a very low-tech  affair and now  as we're seeing today a much more heavy  weaponry on both sides so the  destructive power  is much greater but but ultimately this  is a conflict of identity of territory  between  two nations armenia and azerbaijan and  the outside world is not going to solve  it they're going to kind of  try and lead this horse to water but  that they can't make it drink it's a  region of international importance which  has actually grown more internationally  important  um in the last 25 years because of oil  and gas pipelines  through the region it's a region that  borders iran  so sure it's the european neighborhood  as well as the russian neighborhood and  the us  also got involved in the 1990s  unfortunately what what we've  seen last few years is disengagement by  the us in this issue used to  take much more leading role than it does  now um  and cynically one might say that if  azerbaijan has launched a military  offensive  uh in the last as it looks like  yesterday  one of the reasons it may have done so  is because the us is so disengaged  at the moment there's very little russia  can do um apply a bit of  leverage and pressure um but both sides  in a way have to be able to declare  success after a few days and maybe i've  um you know unfortunately maybe if the  casualties mount up and we're already  probably seeing  at least 100 people dying yesterday  and then that may be a reason for both  sides to think that we can't continue  with this

